// =============================================================================
// MemQL Logic Reference
// =============================================================================
//
// This file is the source of truth for `logic` syntax. It is NOT loaded by
// the engine (the leading underscore on the path tells every walker to skip
// it -- see component/memql/dslfs/walker.go).
//
// `logic` is the behavioral DSL's PURE-DECISION construct. The one-line
// mental model from the behavioral-constructs ADR
// (docs/internal/design/dsl-behavioral-constructs-adr.md, §2):
//
//     logic decides, mutations persist, actions touch the world,
//     queries read, automations orchestrate and react.
//
// A logic reads via queries, composes + decides via control flow, and
// returns a value. It is deterministic and unit-testable by construction:
// the same inputs always produce the same output, with no observable side
// effect. That purity is what lets a logic be replayed and fingerprint-
// verified, reused from any context, and -- soon -- generated by the planner
// against an enforced contract.
//
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Call-graph contract (ADR §2 table) -- enforced by the I2 call-graph validator
// ----------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
//   Construct | May call                                  | May NOT
//   ----------|-------------------------------------------|--------------------
//   logic     | queries, other logic, read-only builtins  | mutations, actions,
//             |                                           | triggers, side effects
//
// Concretely, inside a `logic` body you MAY:
//   - call a `query` (read the graph),
//   - call another `logic`,
//   - call a READ-ONLY `builtin` (an `@executor("integration.*")` whose
//     capability `sideEffectClass` is `read` -- e.g. resolveUser, serviceVersion),
//   - use control flow (`if`, `for ... range`, `coalesce`, arithmetic, shaping).
//
// Inside a `logic` body you MAY NOT:
//   - call a `mutation` (graph write)        -> belongs in an automation step,
//   - call an `action` (external side effect) -> belongs in an automation step,
//   - call a side-effecting builtin           -> reclassify it as an action (I7),
//   - carry a `@trigger`                       -> only automations are reactive.
//
// The single-writer instinct is preserved but RELOCATED to the automation
// step (decide via logic -> persist via one mutation). See ADR §2.1.
//
// =============================================================================
//   1. Logic-level annotations
// =============================================================================
//
// Every annotation goes above the `logic` keyword.
//
//   @description("text")   Human-readable description.                optional
//   @enabled / @disabled   Lifecycle flag (shared across constructs). @enabled
//                          is the explicit-on default (a no-op); @disabled
//                          skips the logic at load.                    optional
//
// NOTE: `@entrypoint` is RETIRED (ADR §2.4). A logic is never a standalone
// entry point. To make a logic reachable from outside the DSL, expose it
// through one of:
//   - a triggered `automation` (react to an event/schedule), or
//   - a `tool` with @handler(type="function", name="theLogic") (governed,
//     arg-schema'd, MCP-exposable external invocation).
// Every logic must be referenced by some logic / query / tool / automation;
// orphans are reported by the dead-logic lint (I4).
//
// =============================================================================
//   2. Anatomy -- args + body + return
// =============================================================================
//
// A logic declares its inputs in an `args { ... }` block and its procedure
// in a `body { ... }` block. The body is a sequence of named statements
// ending in `return <expr>`; the trailing `return` is the function's value.
// Logic functions never write `ctx.output = ...`.
//
// Args field syntax mirrors every other construct:
//   <name> <type> [@required] [@enum("a","b")] [@description("...")]
// Read caller args as `args.X`; engine values are bare top-level names
// (`now`, `actor.X`, `partition`, `config.X`). An arg whose name collides
// with a reserved engine name is rejected at load.

@description("Pure gate check: was the deploy gate green? Defaults to false when the gate result is absent.")
logic deployGatePassed {
  args {
    gate object @required
  }
  body {
    return coalesce(args.gate.passed, false)
  }
}

// =============================================================================
//   3. Single-statement form -- the common case
// =============================================================================
//
// When the decision is one expression, the body is a single `return`. A logic
// may return a constant -- e.g. the canonical engine node-type set the
// deployment bundle iterates over. Engine mesh ONLY (no bff/copresent).

@description("The engine cluster node types the deployment bundle manages.")
logic engineNodeTypes {
  body {
    return ["identity", "cognition", "voice", "agent", "planner", "workbench", "mcp", "voice-agent"]
  }
}

// Conditional shaping note: an inline `if cond { a } else { b }` value is NOT
// expressible today -- a conditional binding's branch bodies must be function
// calls / builtins (see `bootstrapCluster` in dsl/cluster/logic.memql), not
// bare literals. So an env->overlay-path decision is written by routing through
// a builtin or by binding call-bodied branches, e.g.:
//
//   @description("Pick the k8s overlay path for a deployment environment.")
//   logic overlayPathForEnv {
//     args { env string @required @enum("development", "staging", "production") }
//     body {
//       // chosen := if args.env == "development" { localOverlay() } else { envOverlay({ env: args.env }) }
//       // return chosen
//       return overlayPathFor({ env: args.env })   // read-only builtin does the mapping
//     }
//   }

// =============================================================================
//   4. Multi-statement form -- read, compose, decide
// =============================================================================
//
// Intermediate `name := <call>` steps run in dependency order via the
// LogicRunner; the trailing `return <expr>` is the value. Every call here is
// a READ (a query or a read-only builtin) or pure composition -- never a
// mutation or action.
//
// (Imports are illustrative; resolve real constructs in a live file.)
//
//   use cluster.queries.{ existingCluster }
//   use common.builtins.{ serviceVersion }
//
//   @description("Decide whether the cluster still needs bootstrapping, and at what version.")
//   logic bootstrapDecision {
//     args { event object @required }
//     body {
//       existing := existingCluster()                       // query (read)
//       version  := serviceVersion({})                      // read-only builtin
//       needsBootstrap := existing.empty() && args.event.payload.node.type == "bff"
//       return { needsBootstrap: needsBootstrap, version: version }
//     }
//   }
//
// The automation that consumes this decision is where the WRITE happens:
//   step decide { logic bootstrapDecision { event: event } }
//   step record { mutation createCluster { ... } }   // <- one mutation, one write
//
// =============================================================================
//   5. NON-COMPLIANT examples (rejected by the I2 call-graph validator)
// =============================================================================
//
// 5a. Logic that performs a write. A mutation call inside a logic body is a
//     contract violation -- relocate the write to an automation step.
//
//   // INVALID: logic calls a mutation (graph write).
//   logic registerNodeBad {
//     args { event object @required }
//     body {
//       node := createNode({ id: args.event.payload.node.id })  // <- WRITE: rejected
//       return node
//     }
//   }
//
// 5b. Logic that reaches the outside world. An action call belongs in an
//     automation step, not in pure logic.
//
//   // INVALID: logic calls an action (external side effect).
//   logic deployBad {
//     args { ref string @required }
//     body {
//       return action("cloneRepoAtVersion@1") { args { ref: args.ref } }  // <- rejected
//     }
//   }
//
// 5c. Logic that carries a trigger. Reactivity is automations-only (ADR §2.4).
//
//   // INVALID: only automations may carry @trigger.
//   @trigger(event="system.startup")   // <- rejected on a logic
//   logic onStartupBad { body { return true } }
//
// See `_automation.memql` for the construct that legitimately writes,
// acts, and reacts; and `_action.memql` (lands in I6) for the external-
// capability construct.
// =============================================================================
